  "help": "<!--\nHelp text markup guide:\n- Keep visible help content in English.\n- Lines starting with \"# \" become section headings.\n- Lines starting with \"- \" become bullet items.\n- Blank lines separate paragraphs and bullet groups.\n- Inline `text` becomes a code span.\n- Inline **text** becomes bold text.\n- Keyboard tokens such as [Ctrl], [Alt], [Shift], [F1], [F12], [PageDown], and [Esc] become key blocks.\n- Lines starting with \"//\" are ignored.\n- This comment block is ignored by the help renderer.\n-->\n# Editor Overview\n\n- This editor is designed to open and edit txt documents containing D2R tooltip `ÿcX` color codes.\n- Most work is done in the **Editor** pane. It is the main area for reading and editing tooltip text in a human-friendly form.\n- The **Code** pane is a reference area for checking color codes, special characters, and line-break structure.\n- You can edit the Code pane directly, but new users are advised to work primarily in the Editor pane.\n- The layout and the Editor/Code pane ratio can be adjusted to suit your workflow.\n\n# Startup Splash Screen\n\n- A splash screen appears before the main window when the program starts.\n- It remains visible for at least three seconds and closes after the main interface has finished initializing.\n- The splash stays above other windows without taking focus or mouse input away from other programs.\n\n# Basic Workflow\n\n- Open a txt file or drag and drop it onto the program window.\n- Edit the text in the Editor pane.\n- Select characters and apply a color with a color chip or shortcut.\n- Check the actual `ÿcX` color codes and special characters in the Code pane.\n- Review the status bar and any warnings before saving.\n\n# Opening, Saving, and Document Tabs\n\n- The primary supported format is txt.\n- Multiple txt documents can be opened and edited in separate tabs.\n- When multiple files are dragged and dropped, only txt files are opened, in file-name order. Non-txt files in the same drop are ignored.\n- Preferences > General > **Windows Shell Menu** is enabled by default. It adds **Open with D2R Tooltip Editor** to the txt file right-click menu and uses this application icon.\n- If the editor is already running, the shell command opens the selected txt in the existing instance instead of creating a second window. On Windows 11, a classic registry shell command may appear under **Show more options**.\n- [Ctrl]+[N]: New File\n- [Ctrl]+[O]: Open\n- [Ctrl]+[S]: Save\n- [Ctrl]+[Shift]+[S]: Save As\n- [Ctrl]+[W]: Close Current Document\n- [Ctrl]+[Tab] / [Ctrl]+[PageDown]: Move to the next document tab\n- [Ctrl]+[Shift]+[Tab] / [Ctrl]+[PageUp]: Move to the previous document tab\n- When saving, redundant or unnecessary color codes are cleaned conservatively.\n- Characters that affect layout, such as line breaks, spaces, and implemented tabs, are not removed automatically.\n\n# System Tray and Exit\n\n- **Use System Tray (send to tray when closing)** is enabled by default in Preferences.\n- When enabled, the tray icon remains available while the program is running, and the window close button hides the program to the tray instead of exiting.\n- When disabled, the tray icon is hidden while the window is visible, and the close button exits after the normal save checks.\n- Double-click the tray icon to restore the hidden window.\n- Right-click the tray icon to open the **Preferences** and **Exit** menu.\n- **Exit** in the File menu is different from the close button. It offers **Exit**, **Cancel**, and **Minimize to Tray**.\n- **Exit** selected from the File menu, and **Exit** selected from the tray while the window is visible, use the normal unsaved-document checks before fully exiting.\n- When the program is hidden in the tray or minimized, selecting **Exit** from the tray closes it immediately without restoring the window or playing an animation.\n- Both normal and maximized windows slide toward the lower right when hidden and slide back in the opposite direction when restored. A maximized window keeps its size and maximized state during the animation.\n- If the executable is launched while the program is already running, no new instance is created; the existing window is restored.\n- After the program has fully exited, the next launch always starts with the window visible. There is no start-in-tray mode.\n\n# Saving Display Settings\n\n- When saving display settings is enabled, font, font size, line spacing, and alignment are stored in a TTE display-settings block at the end of the txt file.\n- A document containing this block restores those display settings the next time it is opened.\n- Display settings are editor presentation data and are separate from the tooltip text itself.\n\n# Document State and Save Warnings\n\n- A warning can be shown before saving when a document contains actual tab characters or zero-width characters (U+2060).\n- Each warning can be enabled or disabled under Preferences > Save Options.\n- Because U+2060 is invisible, check both the status bar and the save warning.\n\n# Tooltip Alignment\n\n- D2R in-game tooltips generally appear centered.\n- For editing convenience, this editor uses left alignment by default.\n- Use the toolbar to switch between left, center, and right alignment.\n- The default alignment can be changed in Preferences.\n\n# Tab Alignment Work\n\n- Implement/Restore Tabs is intended for left- or right-aligned text.\n- Tab-based alignment may not behave as intended with centered text.\n- Placing actual tab characters directly inside JSON strings used by game files can cause problems.\n- After using tab alignment, check both the save warning and the document-tab indicator.\n\n# Applying Colors\n\n- Select text in the Editor pane and click a toolbar color chip to apply its color code.\n- The Editor preview changes accordingly, while the Code pane shows the actual `ÿcX` code.\n- The default color shortcut is [Ctrl]+[Alt]+the color character.\n- For example, if green is `ÿc2`, press [Ctrl]+[Alt]+[2].\n- When **Default Color for New Input** is enabled, newly typed characters are entered with the default color where possible.\n- When disabled, new input normally follows the preceding color flow.\n\n# Fn Color Shortcuts\n\n- Hover over a color button and press [F1] through [F12] to assign that Fn shortcut.\n- Press the same Fn key again over the same button to clear the assignment.\n- A color with an Fn shortcut no longer uses its [Ctrl]+[Alt]+character shortcut.\n- Clearing the Fn assignment restores the character shortcut.\n- Fn-assigned colors are automatically moved to the front of the toolbar and ordered from [F1] to [F12].\n- Hold [Ctrl] while dragging a regular color button to reorder regular color buttons.\n- Under Preferences > Color Mapping > Character/Fn, **Default** clears only that color's Fn assignment and restores its character shortcut.\n\n# Resetting Color Mapping\n\n- A D2R patch or personal configuration may change how `ÿc` color codes correspond to displayed colors.\n- If custom color mappings have already been saved in Preferences, resetting Color Mapping may be required to see updated system defaults.\n\n# Fonts and Line Spacing\n\n- You can adjust the primary font, font size, and line spacing.\n- Defaults are tuned to resemble in-game tooltips.\n- In addition to bundled fonts, Windows system fonts and directly selected external font files are supported.\n- Frequently used fonts can be pinned as favorites.\n\n# Direct-Path and Fallback Fonts\n\n- Use Editor > Direct Path to select a primary or fallback font.\n- A font selected by direct path is copied into the program's AppData font cache, and the editor uses that copy.\n- When a direct-path font is selected again, a new copy is saved with the date and time appended to the original filename.\n- This prevents an older cached font from being reused when a different font with the same filename is selected.\n- Tooltips containing U+2060 may use the fallback font instead of the primary font in game.\n- When **Fallback font for U+2060 input** is enabled and the document contains U+2060, the Editor pane also uses the fallback font selected in Preferences. If none is selected, the bundled fallback font is used.\n- Enable `Keep ÿc0 in Code Pane` to materialize an otherwise omitted ÿc0 marker for the initial white/default-color run in the Code pane and preserve it when saving.\n\n# Copy and Paste\n\n- [Ctrl]+[Z]: Undo\n- [Ctrl]+[Y]: Redo\n- [Ctrl]+[X]: Cut\n- [Ctrl]+[C]: Copy\n- [Ctrl]+[V]: Regular Paste\n- [Ctrl]+[Shift]+[C]: Copy the font color of the first character in the selection\n- [Ctrl]+[Shift]+[V]: Apply the copied font color to the selection\n- Copy Font Color is available only when text is selected in the Editor, and the context-menu command follows the same rule.\n- Regular paste is available only through [Ctrl]+[V].\n- Paste as Text remains available from the context menu, Edit menu, and toolbar.\n- The regular-paste positions in the context and Edit menus are disabled informational items.\n- Editor copy options control whether color codes are included.\n- You can choose whether line breaks are copied as actual line breaks or as the `\\n` symbol.\n- Unicode can be copied as glyphs, escapes, or omitted.\n- Approximate color paste from externally formatted sources is attempted only with [Ctrl]+[V].\n- Context-menu, Edit-menu, and toolbar text paste reads text only to avoid clipboard permission prompts.\n- Results may differ depending on how the source implements its colors.\n\n# Text Cleanup\n\n- **Text Cleanup** processes only the selected cleanup items.\n- It can selectively remove unnecessary spaces, line breaks, tab characters, and characters that may cause encoding problems.\n- Because cleanup can affect layout, review the selected options before applying it.\n\n# JSON Key Extraction\n\n- **Extract JSON Key** keeps only the selected language-key values from pasted JSON code.\n- The default target is KoKR.\n- The default target language can be changed in Preferences.\n\n# Find, Replace, and Navigation\n\n- [Ctrl]+[F]: Find\n- [Ctrl]+[H]: Replace\n- [Ctrl]+[T]: Go to Line\n- Find/Replace can target the Editor or Code pane separately.\n- Enable `All Tabs` to search or replace across every currently open document tab using the same target mode.\n- The `?` wildcard is supported.\n- Go to Line is useful for quickly locating a specific line in a long document.\n\n# Color Conversion and Unicode Input\n\n- [Ctrl]+[K]: Convert Colors\n- [Ctrl]+[G]: Unicode Input\n- Frequently used values can be managed in the Unicode Input window.\n- Favorite glyphs can also be inserted directly from the context menu.\n\n# Large-File Mode\n\n- Large-File Mode turns on automatically when any of the following thresholds is reached.\n- Total characters: 35,000 or more, measured from the Code pane\n- Visible characters: 15,000 or more, excluding color codes\n- Lines: 1,000 or more\n- To reduce input latency, the Code pane display is paused and Editor processing is limited to roughly 1,000 lines around the current viewport.\n- The complete document and caret position remain in the internal document model. Scrolling loads the relevant area, and clicking moves the caret to that document position.\n- The Code pane is displayed again when the document shrinks or Large-File Mode is disabled. Editing and saving continue normally.\n- Some features may be unavailable or incomplete in Large-File Mode.\n- Editor and Code word wrap are temporarily disabled to protect line-number and caret stability.\n- Line numbers represent logical lines.\n- The color overlay prioritizes click and caret stability over highly detailed color preview.\n- Only lines around the current viewport are rendered in the color overlay, so colors remain visible without a separate on/off option.\n\n# Status Bar\n\n- The status bar shows the current mode, document size, caret position, and action messages.\n- Warnings and errors also appear in the status bar.\n- Check it when working with large documents or invisible characters.\n\n# Language\n\n- Use the **Language** menu next to **Info** to switch between **English** and **한국어**.\n- Korean is the default for new settings.\n- The selected language is applied immediately and restored the next time the program starts.\n- `Korean.lng` and `English.lng` are bundled with the executable.\n\n# Preferences\n\n- Light and Dark modes are supported.\n- Dark mode is the default.\n- The startup theme can be changed in Preferences.\n- Editor and Code pane colors can also be customized.\n- Save options, display-setting storage, default alignment, target language, color mapping, and other key behaviors are configurable.\n\n# User Data Cleanup\n\n- User data can remain even in a portable distribution.\n- This can include preferences, recent-file lists, search history, custom color mappings, custom font caches, and AppData/LocalAppData folders.\n- Preferences > General > **Delete User Data Created by the Program..** removes data created by the application and exits the program.\n- It does not delete the executable, user-edited txt documents, or original user font files.\n\n# Developer Mode\n\n- Developer Mode is enabled or disabled with the **Developer Mode** checkbox in the About window.\n- The checkbox takes effect immediately without a confirmation dialog.\n- Developer Mode lasts only until the program exits.\n- In normal mode, [F12] does not open Developer Tools and may be used as a color Fn shortcut.\n- In Developer Mode, [F12] opens Developer Tools.\n- The F12 color shortcut assignment is retained and only temporarily disabled while Developer Mode is active.\n\n# Developer Reference\n\n- This section is for maintaining the build source, not for normal operation.\n- If a D2R patch changes the connection between `ÿc` color codes and displayed colors, check `DEFAULT_COLOR_PALETTE` and `DEFAULT_COLOR_CODE` in `app/src/colorPalette.js`. Build-source reference.\n- In `DEFAULT_COLOR_PALETTE`, `code` is the D2R color code, `hex` is the editor display color, and `name`/`memo` are UI descriptions. Build-source reference.\n- If the user has already saved custom color mappings in Preferences, resetting Color Mapping may be required to see new system defaults.\n- Some `FocusableWidget` entries in panel layout JSON can define individual `tooltipStyle`/`fontStyle` values. Build-source reference.\n- Setting `newlineHandling` to `standard` reflects the line structure more directly and can be used for left-aligned tooltip layouts. Build-source reference.\n"
}
